* UPGRADE_EDITGATES
* S.64 (owner, 17 Oct 2026).  Keep what the existing accounts have when the edit gates arrive.
*
* The edit gates are OFF by default: a new account cannot save a change to a VOC, a program file or a dictionary until SDSYS gives it
* the right (MODIFY.ACCOUNT VOC-ON / BP-ON / DICT-ON).  But the accounts that exist when this ships have always been able to, and a
* blank in the register reads as denied, so an upgrade that did nothing would lock every one of them out of ED on their programs, of
* BASIC and CATALOG, and of every dictionary edit on the morning after.  The owner's rule for gates that arrive (13 Oct, S.63, and
* the same for these): "existing accounts leave on to preserve their existing situation".
*
* So this sets ACC$VOC.EDIT, ACC$BP.EDIT and ACC$DICT.EDIT to 'yes' ONCE for every USER account in the register (an sdu_ group: the
* account a person normally signs on to) whose field is BLANK, that is, never told.  A field that already says 'no' is somebody's
* decision and is left alone.
*
* GROUP AND OTHER ACCOUNTS ARE LEFT OFF ON PURPOSE (the delivered default is the tighter one: high security is delivered and the
* administrator loosens it).  They are not unreachable: a member of the account's Linux group who signs on to one DIRECTLY - sd -a<name>,
* a batch job run as sd -a<name>, or an API connection to it - gets THAT ACCOUNT'S OWN switches, because the sign-on account decides and
* only a LOGTO from another account leaves the earlier account's gates alone.  So where members need to save to a VOC, a program file or
* a dictionary in a group or other account that way, SDSYS gives it with modify.account <account> voc-on / bp-on / dict-on; nothing
* here does it for them.  SDSYS needs nothing from this program: the administrator passes every gate on a terminal or in a batch job,
* and an API session of SDSYS - which carries no administrator flag - is given the three gates ON in apisrvr (the sdsys account's
* register record has no edit fields, and modify.account refuses sdsys).
*
* ONCE MEANS ONCE.  An administrator who later runs MODIFY.ACCOUNT jack BP-OFF must not find the next install turning it back on, so
* the walk leaves a marker, sdsys/$editgates.done, and does nothing when the marker is there.  The marker is written only after every
* account was written, so a run that stopped half way is repeated whole (a field that now says 'yes' is not blank and is left alone).
*
* RUN gpl.bp upgrade_editgates       set the edit gates for the existing accounts, once
*
* It runs from the installer's upgrade block, as sd -internal, next to upgrade_gates: after UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL and before the
* register is chowned to sdsys.  It prints COMPLETE on a line of its own when, and only when, the register holds what it should; the
* installer matches on that word and nothing else.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 23 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux d7cda81: carried; the include is keys.h and the sdu_ test folds case.  Run by upgrade-gates.ps1 after
*               upgrade_gates.  RELEASE_1.1 170.
* 17 Oct 26 dm  S.64 part 2: the header says that group and other accounts are left off on purpose and what a direct sign-on to one gets,
*               and that sdsys over the API is covered in apisrvr.  Comments only.
* 17 Oct 26 dm  New, from upgrade_gates.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program upgrade_editgates

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   marker = @sdsys:@ds:'$editgates.done'

   if ospath(marker, OS$EXISTS) then
      crt 'The edit gates were set for the existing accounts by an earlier install; nothing changed.'
      crt 'COMPLETE'
      stop
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      crt 'Cannot open the account register: the edit gates were NOT set.'
      stop
   end

   changed = 0
   kept = 0
   failed = 0
   select acc.f to 12
   loop
      readnext reg.name from 12 else exit
      readu rec from acc.f, reg.name else
         continue
      end
      if downcase(rec<ACC$GROUP>[1, 4]) = 'sdu_' then
         if trim(rec<ACC$VOC.EDIT>) # '' and trim(rec<ACC$BP.EDIT>) # '' and trim(rec<ACC$DICT.EDIT>) # '' then
            kept += 1
            release acc.f, reg.name
         end else
            if trim(rec<ACC$VOC.EDIT>) = '' then rec<ACC$VOC.EDIT> = 'yes'
            if trim(rec<ACC$BP.EDIT>) = '' then rec<ACC$BP.EDIT> = 'yes'
            if trim(rec<ACC$DICT.EDIT>) = '' then rec<ACC$DICT.EDIT> = 'yes'
            written = @true
            write rec to acc.f, reg.name on error written = @false
            if written then
               changed += 1
            end else
               failed += 1
               release acc.f, reg.name
               crt 'Cannot write the register record of ' : reg.name
            end
         end
      end else
         release acc.f, reg.name
      end
   repeat

   crt 'Edit gates: switched on for ' : changed : ' existing account(s); ' : kept : ' already had all three set; ' : failed : ' failed'

   if failed # 0 then
      crt 'The gates were NOT all set, so the marker is not written and the next install repeats this.'
      stop
   end

   openseq marker to marker.f then
      weofseq marker.f
   end else
      create marker.f else
         crt 'Cannot write ' : marker : ': the gates are set, but the next install will repeat the walk (harmless).'
         crt 'COMPLETE'
         stop
      end
   end
   writeseq 'S.64: the edit gates were set for the existing accounts' to marker.f else null
   closeseq marker.f

   crt 'COMPLETE'
   stop

end

* END-CODE
